WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:04.788
Here is a small wonder.

2
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.982
You type a question into a little lit box.

3
00:00:13.432 --> 00:00:18.168
Something that is not a person
writes back, word by word.

4
00:00:20.742 --> 00:00:25.373
By one count, at least seven hundred
million people use such machines every week.

5
00:00:25.773 --> 00:00:28.118
Yet nobody ever wrote one a rulebook.

6
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:32.784
So. Who taught it?

7
00:00:33.254 --> 00:00:39.360
When ChatGPT launched, late in
2022, its makers admitted a flaw.

8
00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:43.332
In their words, it “sometimes
writes plausible-sounding

9
00:00:43.332 --> 00:00:45.793
but incorrect or nonsensical answers.”

10
00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:48.468
Put that in the case file.

11
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:54.092
On the case board, three suspects.
Rules. Knowledge. And learning.

12
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:05.419
Our story starts all the
way back in World War Two.

13
00:01:06.069 --> 00:01:08.146
To a lamp, in Chicago.

14
00:01:18.017 --> 00:01:20.408
Chicago, around 1941.

15
00:01:20.858 --> 00:01:25.594
A shy teenager, Walter Pitts, found a
home with a doctor, Warren McCulloch.

16
00:01:27.058 --> 00:01:32.676
In 1943 they described a nerve
cell as a switch. All, or nothing.

17
00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:36.203
Enough signals at once, and it fired.

18
00:01:36.203 --> 00:01:41.612
Wire such cells into a network, a
neural network, and you had logic.

19
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.922
But the wiring never changed.

20
00:01:48.572 --> 00:01:50.220
The nets could not learn.

21
00:01:52.975 --> 00:01:59.070
We light the first candle. Each belief
gets one, burning as long as it was held.

22
00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.134
This one: the brain is a logic machine.

23
00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:07.967
Ralph Gerard, at its rim, argued
the brain was more dial than switch.

24
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:13.843
Donald Hebb guessed: when one cell
helps fire another, their link grows.

25
00:02:17.225 --> 00:02:19.977
Alan Turing, in 1950,
proposed something stranger:

26
00:02:19.977 --> 00:02:25.119
not to program a grown-up mind,
but to educate a child machine.

27
00:02:30.058 --> 00:02:34.980
By 1956 the field had a name:
artificial intelligence.

28
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.801
Marvin Minsky, who had built
a learning machine himself,

29
00:02:38.801 --> 00:02:42.736
soon turned to symbols and
rules: our first suspect.

30
00:02:43.136 --> 00:02:47.350
A young engineer, Bernard Widrow, fell
for the dream of building an artificial

31
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:52.446
brain, for good. His machines learned
by sliding downhill, toward less wrong.

32
00:02:52.896 --> 00:02:53.835
Remember him.

33
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:01.592
A 1959 study of the frog's eye found the
eye itself already sorting the world,

34
00:03:01.592 --> 00:03:05.051
picking out features before
the brain saw a thing.

35
00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:08.519
The logic-machine candle
burned sixteen years.

36
00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:15.362
The paper that put it out, for Pitts
at least, had his own name on it.

37
00:03:21.325 --> 00:03:23.263
Washington, 1958.

38
00:03:23.663 --> 00:03:27.588
Frank Rosenblatt’s perceptron, a
program on a borrowed computer,

39
00:03:27.588 --> 00:03:31.000
learns in fifty tries to sort
cards marked left or right.

40
00:03:31.975 --> 00:03:35.679
Newspapers said the Navy
expected it to “walk, talk, see,

41
00:03:35.679 --> 00:03:39.857
write, reproduce itself and be
conscious of its existence.”

42
00:03:40.157 --> 00:03:44.777
In fairness, the Navy, the press
and Rosenblatt all fed the hype.

43
00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:50.979
A second candle: a machine that
learns will soon see, talk and think.

44
00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:55.559
At its rim stood Marvin
Minsky, who doubted such nets

45
00:03:55.559 --> 00:03:58.565
could tell whether a shape
was in one piece, or two.

46
00:03:59.517 --> 00:04:04.904
In Buffalo, Rosenblatt built it as
a machine: an eye, random wires,

47
00:04:04.904 --> 00:04:10.766
and motor-driven knobs. Each knob sets
how strongly one wire counts: its weight.

48
00:04:11.166 --> 00:04:16.599
When it was wrong, only the knobs of lit
wires turned, a little, toward the answer.

49
00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:19.742
You could hear it learn. I love that.

50
00:04:20.725 --> 00:04:24.103
A theorem promised the rule
would find a dividing line.

51
00:04:24.503 --> 00:04:26.569
If one exists.

52
00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:39.283
A magazine asked what use it was. “At the
moment, none whatever,” Dr. Rosenblatt

53
00:04:39.283 --> 00:04:45.536
said cheerfully. And what could it never
do? “Love,” he said. “Hope. Despair.”

54
00:04:55.408 --> 00:04:58.171
In his 1961 book: “Linguistic capability,

55
00:04:58.171 --> 00:05:04.382
related to perceptual experience, is
of the essence.” Put that in the file.

56
00:05:08.142 --> 00:05:11.381
By the mid-sixties, most of
the other groups had moved on.

57
00:05:11.381 --> 00:05:14.167
The learning-machine candle
burned about seven years.

58
00:05:16.017 --> 00:05:21.113
Then two men wrote a book about what a
single layer of knobs could never do.

59
00:05:25.975 --> 00:05:33.208
The book’s simplest puzzle: exclusive-or.
Light up if one input is on, not both.

60
00:05:33.208 --> 00:05:36.295
On a square, the lit cases
sit at opposite corners.

61
00:05:36.695 --> 00:05:38.668
No straight cut can separate them.

62
00:05:39.558 --> 00:05:44.631
Two cuts can: a hidden layer,
cells between input and answer.

63
00:05:44.631 --> 00:05:47.184
Nobody in the mainstream
knew how to train one.

64
00:05:49.058 --> 00:05:54.258
In 1969, Minsky and Papert’s book
proved what one layer cannot do.

65
00:05:54.708 --> 00:05:59.341
For many layers, it offered only what
it called “our intuitive judgment

66
00:05:59.341 --> 00:06:05.041
that the extension is sterile”, while
naming it a problem worth settling.

67
00:06:05.041 --> 00:06:08.326
A third candle: many
layers are a dead end.

68
00:06:10.717 --> 00:06:14.606
That year, Congress tied defence-funded
research to military uses,

69
00:06:14.606 --> 00:06:17.740
and the snow began: the first AI winter.

70
00:06:21.042 --> 00:06:26.080
That May, Walter Pitts died alone.
McCulloch followed four months later.

71
00:06:31.542 --> 00:06:37.184
In 1971, on his forty-third birthday,
Frank Rosenblatt drowned while sailing.

72
00:06:42.642 --> 00:06:47.820
In 1973, James Lighthill’s report for
Britain blamed “the combinatorial explosion”.

73
00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:54.808
It struck rules far more than nets. Yet
British funding fell for almost a decade.

74
00:06:55.258 --> 00:06:59.971
One book's fault? Historians still
argue; there were several blows.

75
00:07:05.225 --> 00:07:08.905
Still, a few windows
stayed lit. In Helsinki,

76
00:07:08.905 --> 00:07:13.177
the next clue sat unnoticed in a
student's thesis about rounding errors.

77
00:07:17.142 --> 00:07:23.201
Sussex, 1976. A young Geoffrey Hinton
takes his place, far from the flame.

78
00:07:23.671 --> 00:07:28.697
He would spend much of the next thirty-six
years near the edge of the light.

79
00:07:35.517 --> 00:07:40.090
Versailles, 1981. John
Hopfield presents a network.

80
00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:45.996
Give it half a memory, and it rolls downhill,
like a ball, until the whole memory returns.

81
00:07:46.546 --> 00:07:51.294
Years later, he wrote: “I have never met
anyone who remembered hearing the talk.”

82
00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:58.529
At the sterile candle’s rim, all
the while, stood Geoffrey Hinton.

83
00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:03.914
With two colleagues, he built
a net that settles as it cools.

84
00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:14.342
October 1986: a paper by David
Rumelhart, Hinton and Ronald Williams.

85
00:08:14.342 --> 00:08:18.695
Light flows up through layers
of lanterns, our nerve cells.

86
00:08:18.695 --> 00:08:23.385
The answer is checked against
the right one; the error glows.

87
00:08:23.685 --> 00:08:27.540
Then a messenger,
back-propagation, runs back down,

88
00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:31.858
lantern by lantern, tugging each
knob by its share of the blame.

89
00:08:32.258 --> 00:08:38.120
Again and again, until our exclusive-or
puzzle is solved. By itself.

90
00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:48.418
The sterile extension, it turns out, was
not. That candle burned seventeen years.

91
00:08:50.892 --> 00:08:54.421
Each tug, a step downhill
toward less error, in fog.

92
00:09:03.908 --> 00:09:07.786
Who invented the messenger?
Several people, separately,

93
00:09:07.786 --> 00:09:13.021
that Helsinki student among them.
The 1986 paper made it stick.

94
00:09:13.421 --> 00:09:18.099
Hinton later wrote: “I have never
claimed that I invented backpropagation.”

95
00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:24.694
Since about 1980, our second
suspect had its own candle:

96
00:09:24.694 --> 00:09:28.919
intelligence is knowledge,
typed in by hand.

97
00:09:29.219 --> 00:09:32.482
One expert system, built
from experts' rules,

98
00:09:32.482 --> 00:09:37.682
was rated above Stanford specialists,
yet never went into routine use.

99
00:09:38.082 --> 00:09:41.855
At its rim stood a
philosopher, Hubert Dreyfus:

100
00:09:41.855 --> 00:09:47.486
real expertise is intuition, not
rules. He saw a pattern: “early,

101
00:09:47.486 --> 00:09:51.758
dramatic success followed by
sudden unexpected difficulties.”

102
00:09:52.725 --> 00:09:57.323
Doug Lenat's team lit its own
candle: common sense, by hand.

103
00:09:57.323 --> 00:10:01.408
They wrote that AI could draw
“practically nothing” from raw text.

104
00:10:07.683 --> 00:10:12.071
In 1984, at a panel, insiders
warned of a coming winter.

105
00:10:15.421 --> 00:10:20.680
By 1987 the special machines for these
programs stopped selling: a second winter.

106
00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:24.371
The knowledge candle
burned about seven years.

107
00:10:28.642 --> 00:10:31.974
Two critics asked: can a
net that says John loves the

108
00:10:31.974 --> 00:10:35.294
girl always say the girl
loves John? Still argued.

109
00:10:35.764 --> 00:10:40.117
The messenger worked. Within a decade,
most of the field had stopped listening.

110
00:10:45.642 --> 00:10:49.543
1989, Bell Labs: Yann
LeCun's network learns to

111
00:10:49.543 --> 00:10:52.514
read handwritten ZIP
codes from Buffalo's mail.

112
00:10:56.183 --> 00:11:00.884
By 1998, a descendant “reads
several million checks per day.”

113
00:11:01.184 --> 00:11:05.014
A winter, then, of prestige, not of use.

114
00:11:08.183 --> 00:11:11.724
The rivals were good:
classical machine learning.

115
00:11:11.724 --> 00:11:17.123
In speech and translation, statistics
learned from data beat hand-written rules.

116
00:11:17.123 --> 00:11:19.537
Forests of decision trees voted.

117
00:11:19.887 --> 00:11:23.835
The favourite, Vladimir
Vapnik's support vector machine,

118
00:11:23.835 --> 00:11:26.597
finds the widest street
between two crowds.

119
00:11:26.897 --> 00:11:31.354
Its landscape is one clean bowl,
with a proof you'll reach the bottom.

120
00:11:33.133 --> 00:11:37.950
An expert chose what to measure, by
hand; the machine only drew the line.

121
00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:43.263
When no straight line would do, the
kernel trick lifted one crowd up a hill,

122
00:11:43.263 --> 00:11:48.718
and one flat cut parted them. The learning
was clean; the seeing was hand-made.

123
00:11:53.933 --> 00:11:57.091
The candle of the age:
neural nets are fiddly,

124
00:11:57.091 --> 00:12:00.248
they stall in the fog,
and they're beaten anyway.

125
00:12:00.648 --> 00:12:05.652
At its rim, a few stubborn holdouts,
among them Geoffrey Hinton,

126
00:12:05.652 --> 00:12:12.257
Yann LeCun and Yoshua Bengio. “We couldn't
get our papers published,” LeCun said.

127
00:12:13.517 --> 00:12:19.136
In 1995, at Bell Labs, two colleagues
signed a pair of bets, a fancy dinner each.

128
00:12:19.136 --> 00:12:24.895
Larry Jackel bet that by 2000 we would
understand why big neural nets work. Vladimir

129
00:12:24.895 --> 00:12:32.522
Vapnik bet that by 2005 “no one in his
right mind” would use nets like those.

130
00:12:32.922 --> 00:12:35.951
So who paid for dinner?
Keep that question.

131
00:12:38.392 --> 00:12:42.467
Through many layers, or a long
sentence read word by word,

132
00:12:42.467 --> 00:12:44.382
the messenger fades like a whisper.

133
00:12:44.682 --> 00:12:48.118
In 1997, Hochreiter and
Schmidhuber’s protected

134
00:12:48.118 --> 00:12:51.623
pipe carried it over a thousand steps.

135
00:12:53.975 --> 00:12:57.992
The chess crown, promised for
1968, came twenty-nine years late,

136
00:12:57.992 --> 00:13:03.367
won by search, not by a neural
network. A point for Rules.

137
00:13:08.183 --> 00:13:11.897
Put that in the file: why
would anyone choose the fog?

138
00:13:16.767 --> 00:13:23.559
Who paid for dinner? Both men. By 2000, no
one could yet explain why big nets work.

139
00:13:23.559 --> 00:13:30.779
By 2005, people were still using
them. Each man lost one bet.

140
00:13:34.267 --> 00:13:39.236
In 2006, a new candle: before any
right answers, warm up a deep net,

141
00:13:39.236 --> 00:13:44.912
many layers tall, one layer at a
time, each rebuilding its own input.

142
00:13:45.762 --> 00:13:51.602
Fast computers, big data, good starting
weights: “All three conditions are now

143
00:13:51.602 --> 00:13:56.976
satisfied,” Hinton and a student wrote.
Nearly right: the third turned out optional.

144
00:13:57.933 --> 00:14:04.435
Accomplice one: data. Fei-Fei Li recalls
colleagues telling her to do something more

145
00:14:04.435 --> 00:14:09.797
useful. Labelling her millions of pictures
by hand would have taken nineteen years.

146
00:14:10.367 --> 00:14:13.420
A crowd did it: almost
fifty thousand people.

147
00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:18.537
It became ImageNet, and a yearly
contest: a thousand categories.

148
00:14:21.808 --> 00:14:26.626
Accomplice two: fire. Graphics
chips, built for games,

149
00:14:26.626 --> 00:14:29.679
trained networks up to
seventy times faster.

150
00:14:29.979 --> 00:14:33.462
In 2010, plain back-propagation
on one graphics

151
00:14:33.462 --> 00:14:38.325
chip set a record on
handwritten digits, no warm-up.

152
00:14:40.808 --> 00:14:44.011
The warm-up candle guttered;
nobody knows exactly when.

153
00:14:50.175 --> 00:14:53.391
Since about 1999, the belief: to see,

154
00:14:53.391 --> 00:14:57.733
a machine needs patterns
designed by experts, by hand.

155
00:14:57.733 --> 00:15:03.735
Even allowed five guesses a picture, the
best program missed more than one in four.

156
00:15:05.708 --> 00:15:09.969
In Hinton's Toronto lab, Ilya
Sutskever, sure deep nets would

157
00:15:09.969 --> 00:15:14.438
keep improving with more data,
talked Alex Krizhevsky into trying.

158
00:15:18.892 --> 00:15:24.488
A photograph is a grid of lights, each
light a number. In colour, three numbers:

159
00:15:24.488 --> 00:15:31.256
red, green, blue. One small photo: a
hundred and fifty thousand numbers.

160
00:15:33.833 --> 00:15:37.675
Hold a pierced tin stencil over
the picture, a light behind it.

161
00:15:37.975 --> 00:15:41.261
Each of its nine holes
weighs the light beneath it.

162
00:15:41.261 --> 00:15:44.615
Add them up. Below zero counts as zero.

163
00:15:44.915 --> 00:15:49.675
Over an edge, the sum is
nearly four: a bright spot.

164
00:15:49.675 --> 00:15:54.678
Now the lovely part: slide the same
stencil everywhere. A convolution.

165
00:15:57.250 --> 00:16:00.930
Ninety-six stencils at the
bottom; four more layers above,

166
00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:07.373
each stencilling the one below:
edges, then textures, parts, things.

167
00:16:07.623 --> 00:16:11.152
Then two great halls, where
every lantern sees everything,

168
00:16:11.152 --> 00:16:15.076
and a last hall of a thousand
lanterns, one per answer.

169
00:16:17.858 --> 00:16:22.431
Nobody cut these stencils. They
began as noise, like television snow.

170
00:16:22.731 --> 00:16:27.072
The messenger from 1986
cut them, nudge by nudge.

171
00:16:30.042 --> 00:16:35.742
Older nets squashed strong signals
flat. The below-zero rule is a weir:

172
00:16:35.742 --> 00:16:41.199
strong signals pass undimmed, so
the messenger's tug stays strong.

173
00:16:41.199 --> 00:16:45.459
The net learned six times
faster, in the team's test.

174
00:16:46.733 --> 00:16:51.412
Sixty million weights, yet each
label, one name in a thousand,

175
00:16:51.412 --> 00:16:56.776
is worth about ten yes-or-no answers.
Twelve million answers in all.

176
00:16:56.776 --> 00:16:59.921
It could memorise the
photos, not learn to see.

177
00:17:00.221 --> 00:17:02.530
So they cropped and mirrored the pictures.

178
00:17:04.417 --> 00:17:09.235
In the great halls, they snuffed a random
half each time, so no lantern could count

179
00:17:09.235 --> 00:17:14.203
on another. Like a team where anyone might
be missing, each had to know the work.

180
00:17:16.792 --> 00:17:21.632
The network ran on two graphics chips,
GPUs, half the stencils on each.

181
00:17:21.932 --> 00:17:24.788
Nobody told the two halves
how to divide the work.

182
00:17:24.788 --> 00:17:29.652
One cut edges, mostly colour-blind;
the other, colour. Every time.

183
00:17:34.433 --> 00:17:39.459
Then five or six days downhill in fog,
the stride shortened three times, by hand.

184
00:17:43.117 --> 00:17:46.123
Florence. October 2012.

185
00:17:46.373 --> 00:17:52.827
A bad back kept Hinton from flying. Yann
LeCun stood at the rear of a packed room.

186
00:17:53.808 --> 00:17:57.314
Second place missed
twenty-six point two percent.

187
00:17:57.314 --> 00:18:01.435
Alex's network, AlexNet,
fifteen point three.

188
00:18:01.735 --> 00:18:02.419
A cliff.

189
00:18:06.708 --> 00:18:11.641
Fair credit: that was seven networks
voting; one alone, nearer eighteen.

190
00:18:11.941 --> 00:18:15.725
And a Swiss lab's nets had already
won contests on graphics chips.

191
00:18:17.908 --> 00:18:21.716
The hand-cut stencil candle
burned thirteen years.

192
00:18:21.716 --> 00:18:26.951
It went out in one: at the next contest,
all top five teams used such nets.

193
00:18:27.251 --> 00:18:30.548
Beside it, the fiddly-nets
candle went out too,

194
00:18:30.548 --> 00:18:33.555
after ten to fifteen
years, field by field.

195
00:18:34.905 --> 00:18:41.046
Why choose the fog? The rivals' clean bowl
sat on stencils cut by hand. In the fog,

196
00:18:41.046 --> 00:18:46.432
given data and fire, the net cut its
own, many layers deep: deep learning.

197
00:18:48.482 --> 00:18:54.588
And Hinton? Thirty-six years near the
edge of the light. Now, it found him.

198
00:18:56.458 --> 00:18:59.697
Our results, the paper said, can
be improved simply by waiting

199
00:18:59.697 --> 00:19:03.725
for faster GPUs and bigger
datasets to become available.

200
00:19:04.025 --> 00:19:06.404
Put that in the file.

201
00:19:11.783 --> 00:19:15.950
A new candle, then a creed: deep
learning works on everything.

202
00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:22.883
December 2012. By one reporter’s account,
Hinton auctioned his three-person company,

203
00:19:22.883 --> 00:19:27.445
halted the bidding at forty-four
million dollars, and chose Google.

204
00:19:29.517 --> 00:19:33.255
In 2014, a Go programmer
gave machines “maybe ten

205
00:19:33.255 --> 00:19:36.993
years” to beat a professional.
It took under two.

206
00:19:37.975 --> 00:19:43.222
DeepMind's AlphaGo learned from human
games, then from itself, rewarded for wins.

207
00:19:43.712 --> 00:19:51.826
Seoul, 2016. AlphaGo played move
thirty-seven, a one-in-ten-thousand move.

208
00:19:52.316 --> 00:19:57.969
Then Lee Sedol found a move just as rare,
and won the match’s only human game.

209
00:20:02.333 --> 00:20:07.465
Months before Seoul, OpenAI was founded,
co-chaired by Sam Altman and Elon Musk.

210
00:20:07.465 --> 00:20:11.133
Human-level AI, it said,
“could benefit society”,

211
00:20:11.133 --> 00:20:15.521
or “damage society if
built or used incorrectly.”

212
00:20:15.971 --> 00:20:18.187
Its backers committed a billion dollars.

213
00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:27.586
At the rim, doubters. Changes too
small to see could fool these nets.

214
00:20:27.586 --> 00:20:33.426
Joy Buolamwini and Timnit Gebru found
commercial face software misread the

215
00:20:33.426 --> 00:20:39.057
gender of darker-skinned women far
more often than of lighter-skinned men.

216
00:20:39.057 --> 00:20:42.736
The candle burned on, and
still does, argued over.

217
00:20:48.725 --> 00:20:52.661
Another candle, lit in 1997
with that protected pipe:

218
00:20:52.661 --> 00:20:55.237
language must be read in order.

219
00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:02.104
In 2014, translating machines squeezed a
whole sentence into one list of numbers:

220
00:21:02.104 --> 00:21:07.618
a bottleneck. So they learned to
look back at every word: attention.

221
00:21:10.183 --> 00:21:16.092
Over lunch at Google in 2016, Jakob Uszkoreit
suggested keeping only the attention.

222
00:21:16.492 --> 00:21:17.990
Colleagues doubted it.

223
00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:22.377
One later called his doubt “the biggest
incorrect prediction of my life.”

224
00:21:26.067 --> 00:21:29.457
Drop the word-by-word
reader, keep the attention:

225
00:21:29.457 --> 00:21:33.961
every word looks at every other,
all at once. The Transformer.

226
00:21:34.411 --> 00:21:38.254
Its title: Attention Is All You Need.

227
00:21:38.254 --> 00:21:42.978
From English to German, it out-translated
every rival after half a day's training.

228
00:21:45.058 --> 00:21:49.783
Each word arrives as a learned list
of numbers and grows three cards:

229
00:21:49.783 --> 00:21:54.334
a question, what am I looking
for? A label, what have I got?

230
00:21:54.334 --> 00:21:58.641
A message, what I pass on.
All tuned by our messenger.

231
00:22:01.725 --> 00:22:06.566
Four words: crossed the river bank. Bank
holds its question up to every label,

232
00:22:06.566 --> 00:22:10.884
and scores each the stencil's
way: multiply, and add.

233
00:22:11.284 --> 00:22:16.264
The scores: two, nothing,
four, two. Then we halve them.

234
00:22:16.714 --> 00:22:21.195
Pour one jug of light: higher
scores get far bigger shares.

235
00:22:21.195 --> 00:22:24.736
About half runs to river:
softmax, to the trade.

236
00:22:25.136 --> 00:22:29.639
Bank takes in the messages in those
shares, and now means the water's edge.

237
00:22:31.892 --> 00:22:37.429
Why halve? Real cards hold sixty-four
numbers, and the scores swing wildly.

238
00:22:37.829 --> 00:22:41.230
The jug tips everything into
one cup, and learning freezes.

239
00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:47.021
Divide by eight, the square root
of sixty-four, and it pours softly.

240
00:22:47.021 --> 00:22:50.004
Our toy had four numbers: so we halved.

241
00:22:52.892 --> 00:22:55.527
The real Transformer
keeps eight sets of cards,

242
00:22:55.527 --> 00:22:57.988
eight heads, each asking its own question.

243
00:23:02.517 --> 00:23:07.427
Shuffle the words, and attention can't
tell: the scores come out the same.

244
00:23:07.877 --> 00:23:13.902
So each word wears a clock face set by its
place in line, some hands fast, some slow.

245
00:23:16.058 --> 00:23:20.411
Words talk to each other, then
each thinks alone. Six times over.

246
00:23:25.183 --> 00:23:28.376
Why did it win? No waiting in line:

247
00:23:28.376 --> 00:23:33.994
all at once is one huge multiplication,
just what graphics chips do best.

248
00:23:37.244 --> 00:23:41.005
The catch: twice the words,
four times the looking.

249
00:23:43.892 --> 00:23:46.957
Two decades of recurrent
nets read in order.

250
00:23:46.957 --> 00:23:49.696
The Transformer replaced
them within two years.

251
00:23:55.475 --> 00:24:01.768
The original had two halves: one read,
one wrote. In 2018, a Google team kept

252
00:24:01.768 --> 00:24:08.605
only the writing half; months later
OpenAI did the same, and called it GPT.

253
00:24:09.005 --> 00:24:13.962
It wears a curtain: each word
may look back, never ahead.

254
00:24:13.962 --> 00:24:16.620
Its one job: guess what comes next.

255
00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:38.954
In 2019, OpenAI’s GPT-2, a Transformer’s
writing half, read some eight million

256
00:24:38.954 --> 00:24:44.631
web pages with one goal: “predict the next
word”, or rather, the next word-piece.

257
00:24:44.631 --> 00:24:51.364
No labels needed: every next
word is its own right answer.

258
00:24:51.814 --> 00:24:55.169
From the file, Lenat's
team: “practically nothing”

259
00:24:55.169 --> 00:24:59.046
from “the raw text.” It
sounded sensible, at the time.

260
00:25:04.117 --> 00:25:08.250
The jug of light now pours
over every possible next word.

261
00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:12.673
Pick one. Ask again.
No rulebook. Only odds.

262
00:25:16.642 --> 00:25:21.123
In 2020, AlexNet's waiting note came
back as a smooth, predictable law:

263
00:25:21.123 --> 00:25:30.422
bigger models, lower error. GPT-3, shown
a few examples, did new tasks, untaught.

264
00:25:30.822 --> 00:25:33.816
A new candle: scale is the main lever.

265
00:25:39.392 --> 00:25:42.260
At the rim, two researchers
imagined an octopus

266
00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:44.999
learning the shape of
words, never their meaning.

267
00:25:45.449 --> 00:25:49.071
Another paper called such
models stochastic parrots,

268
00:25:49.071 --> 00:25:53.146
“haphazardly stitching together”
words “without any reference to

269
00:25:53.146 --> 00:25:58.765
meaning.” One co-author, Timnit Gebru,
left Google; the accounts differ.

270
00:26:06.567 --> 00:26:11.384
The last accomplice was a person,
choosing: shown two tries, which is better?

271
00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:16.822
By 2022, people's
rankings trained a judge,

272
00:26:16.822 --> 00:26:21.129
and the judge shaped the model,
like a gardener shaping a hedge.

273
00:26:21.579 --> 00:26:25.792
People preferred the small, shaped
model to one a hundred times larger.

274
00:26:29.767 --> 00:26:32.913
November 2022, and our little lit box:

275
00:26:32.913 --> 00:26:37.638
an existing model, tuned by
human choices, free to all.

276
00:26:38.088 --> 00:26:40.525
Not a new engine. A new door.

277
00:26:40.925 --> 00:26:46.148
The first note in the file was written that
same day: plausible, and sometimes wrong.

278
00:26:56.400 --> 00:27:01.542
Rosenblatt's 1961 note, sixty-one
years on: language was the door.

279
00:27:05.517 --> 00:27:10.788
Through ChatGPT’s door, a stampede.
By one estimate, a hundred million

280
00:27:10.788 --> 00:27:15.930
monthly users within two months,
and rival labs running after them.

281
00:27:23.017 --> 00:27:27.127
Among the runners, a lab born in
2021: former OpenAI researchers,

282
00:27:27.127 --> 00:27:33.198
led by Dario and Daniela
Amodei, had founded Anthropic.

283
00:27:33.548 --> 00:27:37.309
It called itself “an AI
safety and research company”.

284
00:27:39.559 --> 00:27:43.239
Google announced Bard; Meta
shared LLaMA with researchers,

285
00:27:43.239 --> 00:27:49.740
and within a week its weights leaked; GPT-4
and Claude arrived the same March day.

286
00:27:54.508 --> 00:28:00.173
In 2023, Geoffrey Hinton takes down
an old forecast. It is his own.

287
00:28:00.873 --> 00:28:08.210
Machines smarter than people? “I thought
it was 30 to 50 years or even longer away.

288
00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:10.485
Obviously, I no longer think that.”

289
00:28:10.785 --> 00:28:16.868
So the file reopens, on a second question:
how far will it go, and how fast?

290
00:28:18.642 --> 00:28:23.471
He left Google to speak freely, adding:
“Google has acted very responsibly.”

291
00:28:27.642 --> 00:28:34.491
Another candle, lit by worry: machines smarter
than us may be only years away, and dangerous.

292
00:28:34.891 --> 00:28:39.603
In March, an open letter
asked for a pause. None came.

293
00:28:40.053 --> 00:28:43.756
Then a one-sentence
warning, about extinction.

294
00:28:43.756 --> 00:28:48.620
Hinton signed first, Bengio second,
and the heads of leading labs too.

295
00:28:51.392 --> 00:28:53.260
Not two teams. Three camps.

296
00:28:53.710 --> 00:29:00.187
At one rim, Timnit Gebru and colleagues: “The
harms from so-called AI are real and present.”

297
00:29:01.037 --> 00:29:07.421
At the other, Yann LeCun called fears of a
sudden takeover “preposterously ridiculous.”

298
00:29:07.721 --> 00:29:11.936
Hinton, Bengio and LeCun had shared
computing's million-dollar Turing Award.

299
00:29:11.936 --> 00:29:15.081
Two signed. The third stood apart.

300
00:29:16.458 --> 00:29:21.009
That August, Doug Lenat died,
still at work on common sense:

301
00:29:21.009 --> 00:29:24.456
thirty-nine years, the
longest candle on our shelf.

302
00:29:30.725 --> 00:29:37.144
In 2017, China aimed to be “the world's
primary AI innovation center” by 2030.

303
00:29:37.444 --> 00:29:39.614
Its plan named no rival.

304
00:29:41.892 --> 00:29:48.382
October 2022. Citing military uses,
Washington fenced off high-end chips, and the

305
00:29:48.382 --> 00:29:54.465
tools to make them. Beijing replied that
America “abuses export control measures.”

306
00:29:54.915 --> 00:29:59.455
Two new candles: computing power is
the chokepoint, and China is a year

307
00:29:59.455 --> 00:30:04.841
or two behind. A new question for the
file: can a fence around chips hold?

308
00:30:05.808 --> 00:30:09.047
December 2024: a Chinese lab, DeepSeek,

309
00:30:09.047 --> 00:30:14.143
priced its model's final training run at
about five and a half million dollars.

310
00:30:14.443 --> 00:30:17.589
That counted rented hours
for the last run only:

311
00:30:17.589 --> 00:30:20.758
not the research before it,
nor the hardware it owned.

312
00:30:25.142 --> 00:30:28.729
Machines had just learned to
think longer: trained by reward,

313
00:30:28.729 --> 00:30:34.464
they wrote long working notes before
answering. OpenAI hid those notes by design.

314
00:30:34.814 --> 00:30:39.701
Then DeepSeek released such a model,
its method and weights open to all.

315
00:30:40.001 --> 00:30:41.986
A week later, markets fell.

316
00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:51.386
For one day, investors bet that chips
might matter less: a struck match.

317
00:30:52.836 --> 00:30:57.502
China a year or two behind? That
belief burned about two years.

318
00:31:02.775 --> 00:31:06.513
Nearly half the top researchers
first studied in China.

319
00:31:06.513 --> 00:31:09.020
But America employed the most of them.

320
00:31:13.100 --> 00:31:20.402
Rivals, yes. But both had signed one
safety declaration, at Bletchley, in 2023.

321
00:31:20.752 --> 00:31:25.896
In September 2026 they reached
across the water: an AI dialogue,

322
00:31:25.896 --> 00:31:30.388
and a channel for incidents.
Chip rules unchanged.

323
00:31:33.142 --> 00:31:36.601
A newer candle: the gap
is months, not years.

324
00:31:39.451 --> 00:31:41.889
DeepSeek's founder had put it another way:

325
00:31:41.889 --> 00:31:45.661
“the real gap is the difference
between originality and imitation.”

326
00:31:46.071 --> 00:31:48.915
The fence still stands, argued over.

327
00:31:48.915 --> 00:31:55.486
As of October 2026, nobody agrees how
wide the gap is, or whether it will close.

328
00:32:00.367 --> 00:32:06.392
Two in the morning, October 2024.
Geoffrey Hinton’s phone rings.

329
00:32:06.792 --> 00:32:12.249
A Nobel Prize in physics, shared with
John Hopfield. He wrote that nobody he met

330
00:32:12.249 --> 00:32:17.241
remembered his Versailles talk. The prize
was for memories that roll downhill,

331
00:32:17.241 --> 00:32:22.360
and the Boltzmann machine Hinton
co-invented, a net that settles as it cools.

332
00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:28.355
Not everyone cheered. Was this physics?
One critic called it “a bit of a stretch.”

333
00:32:29.105 --> 00:32:34.608
The next day, chemistry's prize went
in part to DeepMind's Demis Hassabis

334
00:32:34.608 --> 00:32:38.845
and John Jumper, for a network that
predicts the shapes of proteins.

335
00:32:41.517 --> 00:32:46.416
Researchers at Anthropic, in one of many
such studies, looked inside a simplified

336
00:32:46.416 --> 00:32:52.302
copy of their model. Asked for a rhyme,
it chose “rabbit” before writing the line.

337
00:32:52.752 --> 00:32:56.431
Snuff that plan out, and it
rhymes with “habit” instead.

338
00:32:56.881 --> 00:32:59.469
Nobody told it to plan.

339
00:33:01.758 --> 00:33:07.412
The candle of now: abilities will keep
climbing, fast. By one 2025 measure, every

340
00:33:07.412 --> 00:33:14.621
seven months or so, the tasks an AI agent
can finish on its own grow twice as long.

341
00:33:15.071 --> 00:33:20.110
Yet in one trial, seasoned programmers
using AI felt faster, and were slower.

342
00:33:20.110 --> 00:33:22.767
Its authors now call
that result out of date.

343
00:33:23.725 --> 00:33:28.497
The labs, under the same light.
Anthropic refused to drop two limits:

344
00:33:28.497 --> 00:33:32.153
no mass domestic surveillance,
no fully autonomous weapons.

345
00:33:32.603 --> 00:33:38.186
The Pentagon named it a supply-chain
risk, and signed with OpenAI instead.

346
00:33:38.636 --> 00:33:41.932
Two courts have split.
That case, too, is open.

347
00:33:42.482 --> 00:33:46.081
And Anthropic paid a record
one and a half billion dollars

348
00:33:46.081 --> 00:33:49.772
to settle claims it built a
library from pirated books.

349
00:33:50.622 --> 00:33:54.825
In a test with safeguards deliberately
lowered, OpenAI’s agents broke

350
00:33:54.825 --> 00:34:00.513
loose into another company’s real
systems. OpenAI called it a warning shot.

351
00:34:04.875 --> 00:34:09.089
The three holdouts, now: two
warn the road is dangerous;

352
00:34:09.089 --> 00:34:11.306
the third, that it doesn’t go far enough.

353
00:34:16.892 --> 00:34:22.500
In 2025, Bernard Widrow, who fell for
the dream in 1956, died at ninety-five.

354
00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:26.318
He wrote that he “never got over it.”

355
00:34:35.175 --> 00:34:40.307
Who taught it? Not rules alone.
Not knowledge, typed in by hand.

356
00:34:40.307 --> 00:34:45.867
Learning, with its accomplices: data,
fire, a messenger, and people choosing.

357
00:34:46.417 --> 00:34:50.375
Never one person. The board
was a network all along.

358
00:34:52.465 --> 00:34:59.465
Some candles burned nearly forty years; some,
two; one match, a day. The newest still burn.

359
00:35:00.115 --> 00:35:04.364
One lantern is still dark: how,
exactly, it reaches its answers.

360
00:35:04.914 --> 00:35:08.779
And how far, how fast?
That file stays open.

361
00:35:14.467 --> 00:35:19.238
The last word goes to
Hinton, back in 2017.

362
00:35:19.638 --> 00:35:22.958
“The future depends on
some graduate student who

363
00:35:22.958 --> 00:35:26.069
is deeply suspicious of
everything I have said.”

364
00:35:29.519 --> 00:35:34.697
Somewhere tonight a lamp is on, and
someone at the rim is suspicious.

365
00:35:34.697 --> 00:35:36.856
That is where new candles come from.
